<p>The <a href="https://nationalhumanitiescenter.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Humanities Center</a> (NHC) recently announced its fellows for the 2026-27 academic year, and two philosophers are among them.<span id="more-57062"></span></p>
<p>They are <a href="https://cas.gsu.edu/profile/s-m-love/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">S.M. Lov</a>e (Georgia State University) for the project, &#8220;Freedom from the Market: A Kantian Account of Socioeconomic Rights,&#8221; and <a href="https://dornsife.usc.edu/james-van-cleve/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">James Van Cleve</a> (University of Southern California) for the project, &#8220;Roderick Chisholm and Philosophy in America, 1950–2000.&#8221;</p>
<p>There were 29 fellows in all. One might notice from looking at the <a href="https://nationalhumanitiescenter.org/the-national-humanities-center-announces-2026-27-fellows/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">whole list of them</a> that 8 of the fellows are in history departments. Why just 2 philosophers but 8 historians? Why do philosophers seem underrepresented among interdisciplinary or general humanities fellowships and grants?</p>
<p>The answer is that philosophers tend toward disciplinary insularity. This may be changing somewhat (see, for example, <a href="https://dailynous.com/2026/04/02/philosophy-more-empirical-than-ever/">this</a>) but I suspect it will remain an obstacle for a while.</p>
<p>Rather than provide a definition of &#8220;disciplinary insularity&#8221;, what I offer below is some advice, based on my experiences working on interdisciplinary evaluation panels and my conversations with grant and fellowship administrators over the years, on how philosophers can try to overcome it or counter its effects when applying for humanities fellowships and grants (HFGs). Others are welcome to add their own advice in the comments.</p>
<div id="attachment_20204" style="width: 548px" class="wp-caption aligncenter"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-20204" class=" wp-image-20204" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/kracov-david-book-of-life.jpg" alt="" width="538" height="572" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/kracov-david-book-of-life.jpg 750w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/kracov-david-book-of-life-282x300.jpg 282w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2018/07/kracov-david-book-of-life-400x425.jpg 400w" sizes="(max-width: 538px) 100vw, 538px" /><p id="caption-attachment-20204" class="wp-caption-text">David Kracov, &#8220;Book of Life&#8221;</p></div>
<h4><em>1. Apply</em></h4>
<p>You cannot win if you do not play. Philosophers appear to apply for HFGs at a lower rate than scholars in other disciplines. If you look at the recent NHC fellows, it would be a mistake to infer from the fact that 4 times more historians than philosophers were selected that any given philosopher applicant is at a disadvantage, or that the organization is somehow biased against philosophers. I am reliably informed that many, many more historians tend to apply for these kinds of fellowships and grants than philosophers.</p>
<h4><em>2. Explain Importance &amp; Interestingness</em></h4>
<p>Sometimes, the importance and interestingness of a philosophical question or thesis or figure, while quite clear to people trained in philosophy, is not at all apparent to those not trained in philosophy, and for fellowships like these, the majority of your application&#8217;s evaluators, particularly at the final stages, will not be philosophers. So you need to make the case for the importance and interestingness of your project to non-philosophers. One thing to note about this is that the standards philosophers tend to have for whether a philosophical question is interesting are much lower than the standards non-philosophers tend to have. After all, philosophers are disposed to find philosophy interesting, and for us, it may be that all you need to do is show us that there is an actual philosophical question there, and we&#8217;ll say, &#8220;Huh. Yeah, that&#8217;s something interesting to figure out.&#8221; But this is not true for others, especially when those others are deciding to whom to award fellowships and funding.</p>
<p>Because projects vary so widely, it is difficult to give detailed advice about how to explain the importance and interestingness of your project to non-philosophers. But one thing I very strongly recommend is having several friends and colleagues in several other disciplines review your project description, and then have conversations with them in which you try to determine whether they understood the project, whether they were able to see why it would be important or interesting to philosophers, whether they themselves found it important or interesting, and what your project reminded them of in their home disciplines (related to #6 and #7, below).</p>
<h4><em>3. Don&#8217;t Oversimplify</em></h4>
<p>Dumbing things down may seem like a natural way to explain your project to non-experts, but it is a mistake to do that. The evaluators are fellow scholars all trained to be able to decipher difficult texts: they can understand explanations that are not oversimplified, provided that they&#8217;re clear and contextualized. Oversimplified explanations of projects risk coming off as explanations of overly simplistic projects.</p>
<h4><em>4. Real Stories Are Helpful </em></h4>
<p>HFG evaluators are people, and people like stories. Some of the strongest applications for HFGs include compelling, if brief, true stories that introduced or illustrated the subject of the applicant&#8217;s project. Such stories make things interesting, can demonstrate real-world relevance, and serve an explanatory function at the same time, and they will feel richer for being real. When considered alongside real world examples, the thinness of philosophy&#8217;s familiar thought experiments, as useful as they may be, becomes more noticeable, and that can bleed into evaluations of the importance of a project.</p>
<h4><em>5. Show Your Project Reaches Beyond Itself</em></h4>
<p>For an HFG, your application may be strengthened if you can show that your project reaches beyond itself, and perhaps beyond philosophy, in its focus or research materials or implications. This not only helps with showing its importance and interestingness, but with explaining its potential synergies. For some HFGs, evaluators may find it relevant to their assessment of your project how (and how much) you might benefit from interacting with scholars outside of your discipline, as well as how (and how much) you might benefit them.</p>
<h4><em>6. Bibliographies Are Important</em></h4>
<p>My impression is that philosophers pay less attention to bibliographies than scholars in other humanities&#8212;at their peril. If your project reminds an evaluator of relatively well-known authors or well-studied subjects in their home discipline, they may check your bibliography to see whether you included works by those authors or on those subjects. If you haven&#8217;t, there is a risk that you will appear insufficiently prepared to embark on your project, or that your project will appear to be flawed. You cannot count on the evaluator thinking, &#8220;this is just how they do things in philosophy&#8221; or &#8220;I can see why a philosopher would not include this.&#8221; So in preparing your application for an HFG, it behooves you to research how a version of your question, or something obviously related to your question, has been treated in other humanities disciplines. Who in other disciplines has written about it? What have their main concerns been? Find that out and then add the appropriate citations to your bibliography.</p>
<h4><em>7. Show Your Project Is a Humanities Project</em></h4>
<p>Sometimes HFG evaluators will ask, &#8220;How is this project a project in the humanities?&#8221; You should ask that of your project, too. Now you may be thinking, &#8220;(1) This is a philosophy project. (2) Philosophy is a discipline in the humanities. (3) Therefore, this is a humanities project.&#8221; That is not a helpful way of taking up this question. For one thing, you cannot assume that evaluators will have an inference like that in mind. For another, premise 2 is questionable: as an organizational claim it&#8217;s true but superficial; as a substantive claim, though, its breadth makes it suspect. Philosophers themselves have struggled with philosophy&#8217;s place among other disciplines, and one way of interpreting the development of the analytic/Continental division is that it came to be about whether philosophy is more like a humanities discipline or more like the sciences. In any event, sometimes it is not obvious why a project is a humanities project; if you (or one of your colleagues outside of philosophy) think this may be true of your project, you should probably include in your application something that makes it clear how, in its methods and subject matter, your project is a humanities project.</p>
